  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1200 PM CST Fri Nov 20 2026\n\nHigh pressure to our east yields a warm advective southeast to\nsouth flow thru the remainder of the week. This will translate\nto a continuance of above normal temperatures, as highs run\n10-20 degrees above climo, topping out in the lower half 70s \ndaily for most of us right on thru the weekend. \n\nA cold front's approach and passage Sunday brings our next\nchance of rain. While the models have been consistently lacking\nmoisture and overall qpf output, a quick burst of 1/4\" rainfall\nfrom a stronger shower or storm looks possible. A nose of 60F \ndew points approaches our southern border in a narrow sliver \nahead of the front, helping MUCAPE pinch in a potential couple \nhundred J/KG2 as a 40 KT H5 jet axis swings in with the parent\ntrof/energy well displaced from our FA. The NBM picks up on it \nwith a slgt chc mention of thunder entering our Ozarks and \npropagating within the ribbon toward Fulton Co KY upon the \nfront's advance.\n\nAfter fropa, the bigger impact comes from the Manitoban airmass\nthat will be filtering down/across the Ohio Valley over the\ncourse of the Thanksgiving holiday week. We see our temps\nnosedive thru the more seasonal 50s Monday-Tuesday, before\nlocking in up to around 10F below normal in the 40s for both \nsides of/including the holiday to finish out the week cold.\n\nThe models still diverge at this writing with respect to the\nholiday pcpn chances. The ECMWF maintains a glancing approach\nwhile the GFS doubles down on more vigorous pcpn chances. We\nwill have to keep a close watch upon its impacts as critical\ntime of day temperatures may introduce some light wintry\npotential \"mention\", although with all the recent warmth we do \nnot foresee much significance impact-wise even if a wetter \nsolution becomes more viable.",
